<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saturn (mythology)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saturn was an ancient Roman god of agriculture and wealth, identified with the Greek Kronos, whose festival, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia">Saturnalia</a>, was held in December. His temple in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a> housed the state treasury. In Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> the Punic god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a> was worshipped as Saturn, and his sanctuaries, with thousands of votive stelae, were among the most important in the region until the 4th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saturn-mythology.jpg" alt="Saturn (mythology)" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saturn_with_head_protected_by_winter_cloak,_holding_a_scythe_in_his_right_hand,_fresco_from_the_House_of_the_Dioscuri_at_Pompeii,_Naples_Archaeological_Museum_(23497733210).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Saturn was described as a god of time, generation, dissolution, abundance, wealth, agriculture, periodic renewal and liberation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> kept the memory of a remote time in which Saturn and Janus reigned on the site of the city before its foundation, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> was called mons Saturnius. After the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> he was conflated with the Greek Titan Cronus, whose myths were adapted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature, and as early as the 3rd century BCE Jupiter was called his son. His consort Ops, equivalent to Rhea, was a later association, since the goddess was originally paired with Consus; earlier was his link with Lua, a goddess of destruction and loosening who received the bloodied weapons of enemies killed in war.</p><p>Ancient writers offered several explanations of the name. Varro derived it from satus, sowing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s Balbus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">satis</a>, because Time is satiated by the years; a modern alternative connects it with the Etruscan god Satre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In Roman legend Saturn arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> dethroned and fugitive after his son Jupiter usurped him, was received by Janus and brought agriculture and civilisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> describes his reign as a golden age of peace under which he gathered the unruly fauns and nymphs and gave them laws. The temple of Saturn at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a> was consecrated in 497 BCE and housed the state treasury throughout Roman history; a row of columns from its last rebuilding still stands. His statue was filled with oil and had its feet bound with wool, which was removed only during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia">Saturnalia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia">Saturnalia</a>, held from 17 to 23 December, marked the harvest and sowing. Social restrictions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> were relaxed, and its revels were meant to recall the lost golden age before Saturn's rule was overthrown. Sacrifices to him were made in the Greek rite with the head uncovered, unlike those of other major deities, a custom ancient commentators linked to the god's own veiled image. In the 3rd century CE and later, gladiatorial munera were given near the festival, which Christian writers criticised as a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>.</p><p>Little evidence exists for his cult in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> equated him with the Carthaginian god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal-hammon">Baal Hammon</a>, to whom children were sacrificed, and the poet Tibullus called the Sabbath Saturni dies, from which Saturday derives. The identification with Baal Hammon gave rise to the African Saturn, a cult that was popular until the 4th century and had the character of a mystery religion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saturn (mythology)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Saturn</b>, the Roman form of the Punic Baal Hammon, was the leading god of the countryside before Christianity spread rapidly in the second and third centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The old one was Saturnalia, held in honour of <b>Saturn</b>, a god of agriculture and wealth whose temple in the Roman Forum housed the state treasury.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
